On 2006-07-23, John H. <JohnWH@gmail.com> wrote:
> ChocoCid@gmail.com wrote:
>> There's no reason to "get rid of pudding farming".  If you're going to
>> have monsters that can split endlessly (ie: don't respect extinction),
>> then you need to realize that it is going to be possible to "abuse"
>> this- except it's not really abuse.
>
> It's abuse because it's outside the concept of the game, in which the
> only true limiting factor is item scarcity.  Pudding farming not only
> only allows the possibility of getting an unlimited number of items,
> but it's also relative simple to abuse it with a macro.

I think the true limiting factor is patience, not item scarcity ---
the spell of Create Monster combined with the Eye is, if a bit more
time-consuming than pudding farming, no less item-generating --- but
the potential for macro abuse is an excellent point.

>> Pudding farming is no different than running in circles for a couple
>> hours fighting random battles to level up in a console RPG- it's a task
>> that's done solely for the improvement of your character and has no
>> relevant to the plot/progression of the game.
>
> Don't compare Nethack to those lesser games!  

...this is a bit of a troll, don't you think? :)

> You should understand
> that Nethack contains many such features (most of them obviously more
> intended than pudding farming) that have inspired people to cry abuse
> and others to defend them, and over time they've all been brought more
> in line with game balance: they made it impossible to wish for more
> wishes, each nurse eventually disappears, in/succubuses will always get
> a severe headache after a while, polymorphed items vanish due to
> shuddering vibrations and golem creation and there's also the matter of
> polymorph charges, and so on.  There is no reason pudding farming
> should be any different from these.

All of these things can still be used to make your character absolutely
ridiculously thrudly munchkin powerful, they just take a lot longer. Well,
maybe not wishing for more wishes, but you can reverse genocide more
nurses, same-rac sacrifice up more foocubi, farm (pudding or traditional)
up more items, wish for the PYEC, &c. Granted eventually your wand will
explode and you will need to find another one but that is only a matter
of time. This in my mind argues for making pudding farming more difficult
or even just less scriptable, not removing it entirely. 

I don't have a good suggestion of how to do this and prefer not to make
a bad one. It seems to me that the Nethack philosophy is "Make it easy to
do it wrong, make it hard to do it right, and make trying too hard after
a certain point have diminishing returns." The returns on farming are
(in my opinion, anyway, I tried it) diminishing for an already-kitted-out
character; they are perhaps too great for a weak one.

>> People should stop complaining about pudding farming- any game that has
>> the depth of Nethack is going to have things like pudding farming.
>> Just accept it as a part of the game and move on.  If you get rid of
>> pudding farming, people will just do something else instead. (daytime
>> gremlin farming anyone?)
>
> I believe gremlins cannot drop random items, since they're smaller
> monsters.

You could farm demons that summon other demons, unless that respects
extinction? You could farm trolls (they death-drop every time)? Maybe none
of these methods are quite as blatant as pudding farming but if you are
only trying to preserve item scarcity you are going to run into a whole
lot of ways to get more items. I think the problem with pudding farming
(for those who think there is one; I'm undecided) is the ease of getting
items, not the items themselves.

Anyway, those are my thoughts...

